 | travelinn.test.js | 3 | TRAVEL-INN - WHAT THE INNS/CAMP OUT TOGGLE ACTUALLY DOES, MEASURED ON BOTH SIDES (2026-09-22, DragynDance: "when you use instant travel the game ignores the option to camp or rest at an inn"; Mac: "there's a toggle to rest at inns and stuff during travel. Why doesn't it work?"). IT WORKS OFFLINE AND IS INERT ONLINE, and neither half was written anywhere an execution could check. DRIVEN OUT OF THE REAL POPUP rather than argued from source, because the chain is four modules long - refresh -> travel.js's per-pixel charge -> the trip cost -> the host's arrival minute - and reading any one proves nothing about the other three. OFFLINE the toggle is fully live: 2080 minutes and 10 gold at an inn against 2420 and free camping out, the classic `(300 * thisMove) >> 8` charge for not paying for a bed. ONLINE BOTH CONSEQUENCES COLLAPSE TO ZERO: WORLD5 makes the clock the world's so the trip takes no world time (world.js's arrival is `sharedClockOn() ? worldMinutes() : worldMinutes() + computed.minutes`) and OL2 pays no night because there are no nights - 0 days and 0 gold whichever way it is set, so on the INSTANT path the choice really is ignored, exactly as reported. The difference between that and the silent no-ops fixed beside it is that the player is TOLD: ONLINE_TRAVEL_LINE is drawn in both skins. AND Offline the toggle still decides whether the trip is WALKED or instant (isPlayerControlledTravel reads it); online (TRAVEL-ONLINE, 2026-10-03) the room holds the Inns dial, so a trip over land is walked by inns and by camp alike, which the row pins too. The pin says what is LIVE as well as what is not, so a later slice that makes an online trip spend time moves the online rows while the offline rows hold - the signal to come back and decide what an inn means when the nights belong to everyone. 6 mutants, 6 dead. LIVED1 (2026-09-29): online the trip's nights pass on the traveller's own clock, so the inns are slept as they are paid and the line says whose days they are. |
 | campground.test.js | 6 | CAMP-GROUND + NOTICE-SPAM (2026-09-22, Mac: "Did you tackle camping not working in the world because of flat tarren? And the enhanced notifcation spam?"). THE CAMP PROBE ASKED THE WRONG DOOR, and this is why camping was IMPOSSIBLE rather than merely mute (CAMP-SILENT was the other half). `collider.raycast` walks the TRIANGLE BUCKETS alone; outside, the ground is not a mesh - it is `heightAt`, the terrain sampler applied to the capsule and nowhere else - so a ray cast straight down from a player standing in open country hits NOTHING, campSpot answered a null ground, and campDecision refused with "There is no level ground here" on the flattest meadow in the Iliac Bay, everywhere outdoors, for every kit. THE DOOR THAT FIXES IT IS TWO DAYS OLD AND WAS BUILT FOR THE SAME MISTAKE: `surfaceHit` (MAC-BUG W5, Mac: "blood doesn't work outside"), whose own note names this caller without knowing it - "a caller that reads 'nothing' as 'no surface' is right indoors and silently wrong in the whole outdoors". Blood was moved onto it; camping was not. It answers whichever is NEARER, so a camp under a walkway still finds the walkway, and an older collider keeps the bucket probe rather than falling to null. A sweep of the other downward probes found no third victim (enemyMotor already pairs its ray with heightAt; the interior ones are meshes). ALSO NOTICE-SPAM: a toast system that stacks one line ten times is wrong whatever posted it - the classic column SCROLLS so a repeat costs a row and passes, but the enhanced skin draws a PLATE per row (ENH-NOTICE3), so ten copies of "You are freezing" are ten plates and nothing else can be read. An identical line already at the BACK of the queue now refreshes that row's dwell instead of adding a second, keeping its `id` so the plate is refreshed rather than flickering out and back. Deliberately the back ONLY: "You are hit" twice with something between them is two blows and both belong on screen. The producers are unchanged - DEATHLOOP2 closed the loop that was making them - this closes the ring that was willing to draw them. 7 mutants, 7 dead. |
 | menu_pointer_relock.test.js | 3 | MENU-RELOCK - THE CAMERA COMES BACK ON THE KEY THAT CLOSED THE WINDOW (2026-09-22, Fedu on Discord: a 1-3 second delay before the camera answers the mouse again after closing a menu). POINTER LOCK IS ONLY GRANTED INSIDE A USER GESTURE: the browser hands `requestPointerLock` out on the keypress the player just made and refuses it anywhere else, silently, with nothing retrying - so the look stays dead until the player's NEXT gesture happens to reach a relock arm. The delay is not a timer, it is however long it takes you to press something else. THE PORT ASKED A FRAME LATE: `makeLookGate` is the per-host reconciler and it runs on the FRAME, seeing the overlay slot drain and relocking on the next call, which is a new task with no user activation left. The fix is not a faster gate - it is the four hosts (standalone dungeon, standalone interior, town talk, and the world host's interior and dungeon arms) asking inside the key handler that closed the window, on BOTH edges, because a two-phase window like the automap closes on key-UP and that release is a gesture too. THE FIRST TWO PINS EXECUTE the mechanism and the gap: a real request is made and asks for `unadjustedMovement` (MAC1's raw device counts), a Chrome-shaped rejection is swallowed rather than fatal, an activated cursor survives the relock (U45's precedence), and the GATE IS DRIVEN to show that nothing has asked at the moment of the key and one request lands a frame later - the bug, measured - while PL3's grace keeps the host's in-gesture request from being torn down by a stale frame. The third holds the four call sites by SHAPE, because the gesture itself needs a browser: the incoming patch had put `const hadOverlay` BETWEEN `if (mode === 'interior') {` and its routeKey call, which broke U43's one-dispatch law in qs7_one_dispatch and flagsweep, so the read is hoisted above the mode ladder and pinned there. ONE MUTANT FOUND A HOLE IN THIS FILE'S OWN PIN rather than in the source: `relock` appears twice in world.js - the pause door's and the host literal's - so a whole-file match let the seam be renamed and still passed, while `host.relock?.()` is optional-chained and a missing seam is SILENT. It reads the literal's slice now. 10 mutants, 10 dead. |
