t and applies at once, none for a refused one, the no-shell and no-modules words. THE KEYS ARE THE PLAYER'S: an action given another key answers on it at once and not on the old, kept and read again at the next start; a key another action has refused by name; not a key, no such action, Ctrl, a repeat or a text field never; the panel holds every key while open; null puts the module's key back; a key handler that keeps throwing switches its module off. THE KEYS PANEL: a key for people, Shift+F1 no gameplay default, the panel's own key and the menus' refused, the keys held and the pointer freed, the game's bus and the Modules card reaching it. PINS' EXIT: nothing shown until the player pins it on the map, and no key of its own; a click near where they came in is the exit, snapped to it; the map's own exit mark is the exit; its flag taken away clears it; F3 cycles it between the quest and the pins, and a destination set later leaves it; reaching it gives the compass back; a new place forgets it. mapPick's fifth argument, exit, pinned in the table and at the window's seam. THE KINDS OF MARK: questMark's fourth argument says what the compass marks (the quest, the kind a module named with retarget, a plain place for junk, null for nothing); pins names its pin, destination and exit; the waypoint gives each kind its own colour apart from the quest's gold, and a word. THE KNOWN WAY: up a walked stair to a mark on the floor above, round by the stair; to a teleporter's near end first, and none back through it - only as far as the floor the player can follow reaches toward the goal; nothing off the walked cells; up a climb whose heights stand stacked in one cell (live, Privateer's Hold: without it the floor above was cut off); the trail turns to it only where its floor's own search does not reach; the dungeon hands its map's trail and steps to the probe. THE TRAIL CLIMBS: a hump of 0.82 m a metre, never a ledge or a wall; a blocked start gives way to the next cell; the level is the floor under the feet. THE REGION MAP'S WAYPOINTS: one followed before the module looked waits in the cycle; one followed anew on the map takes the compass, the waypoint's kind and its name; F3 cycles the quest, the destinations and the waypoints; the open world's tile step keeps the waypoint on its spot and moves the destinations with it (no new place); arriving gives the compass back; unfollowed on the map it is let go, and followed again it is new; indoors the quest, and outside the waypoint taken up again; the open world hands the followed waypoints in its own frame, outdoors alone, through the probe. A PLACE ON ITS OWN FLOOR: the exit keeps the floor the player came in on and the compass is told it; standing under it on another floor is not reaching it; a dungeon map's pin keeps the floor the map says (mapPick's sixth argument, dy); a teleporter's jump, landing at the far end the map has just recorded, is no new place; leaving forgets it; retarget hands the height on; a map pin near the way in stays where it was placed (never snapped to the exit), on the floor under it. A place taken away that is not followed leaves the compass where it is. THE COMPASS'S COLOUR: a module may colour the compass's mark ('#rrggbb'; the last ask by an enabled module wins, junk or null the game's own); the HUD reads it as it draws the mark; the guide trail asks its look's colour while it is on and gives it back off. Campaign: ten mutants of the bus, the host, the shell's door and the event table, each red. |
